{
  // Report Crew - stage three of the Deep Research Flow.
  //
  // Renders a fact-checked AnalysisResult into the finished investment
  // briefing. Two sequential tasks: a writer produces the draft, then a style
  // reviewer corrects it against the house guide. Sequential because the
  // reviewer needs the draft; no async, so no barrier task.
  //
  // What these agents are NOT responsible for, and why:
  //   - The Sources appendix. Built in Python from the verified claim list.
  //     A model writing its own would be writing URLs from memory.
  //   - The funding table. Rendered in Python from structured FundingEvent
  //     data, exactly as models.py says it should be: "kept structured so the
  //     Report Crew can render this as a deterministic markdown table instead
  //     of asking an LLM to format numbers correctly from prose."
  //   - fact_check_status. Set by the Flow from the citation gate's result.
  // Everything left is prose, which is the only part worth an LLM.
  //
  // NOTE: both agents are toolless by design, which forces a model choice -
  // see the note in agents/report_formatter.jsonc and CLAUDE.md known gap #3.
  //
  // Note on {"python": ...} refs: they resolve relative to THIS directory, so
  // they point at report_refs. See report_refs.py for why.
  "name": "report_crew",

  "agents": [
    "report_formatter",
    "style_reviewer"
  ],

  "tasks": [
    {
      "name": "format_report_task",
      "agent": "report_formatter",

      "description": "Write the investment committee briefing on {topic}, using the analysis below and following the house style guide exactly.\n\nHOUSE STYLE GUIDE:\n{style_guide}\n\nA PAST BRIEFING IN THE HOUSE STYLE, for calibration - match its tone, density, and length rather than re-deriving them from the guide alone:\n{sample_report}\n\nTHE ANALYSIS TO WRITE UP:\n{analysis_block}\n\nTHE VERIFIED FUNDING FIGURES, for your reference only:\n{funding_table}\n\nDo NOT reproduce this table, or write any markdown table of your own. It is rendered from structured data and inserted into your 'Where Capital Is Flowing' section automatically. Your job in that section is the interpretation underneath it: what the pattern of funding implies about timing, stage, and which investor types are active. An absence of capital is itself a finding worth stating.\n\nDo not restate individual round amounts in that prose. The table already gives every figure, and a sentence repeating them in a different currency or rounding contradicts the table directly above it - a draft once did exactly this, printing 'EUR 32 million' under a table reading $36M. Write about the pattern, not the numbers.\n\nRules specific to this task:\n\n1. The title is the investment question, not the topic. 'Should Northbridge Increase Sourcing Activity in Small Modular Reactors?' - not 'Small Modular Reactor Market Overview'.\n\n2. Use the executive summary from the analysis as the Executive Summary section, essentially verbatim. It was written by the strategist who took the position and has already been through the fact-check gate. Do not re-argue it.\n\n3. Include a 'Where Sources Disagree' section ONLY if the analysis lists tensions. If that list is empty, omit the section entirely rather than padding it - the guide is explicit that omitting beats padding.\n\n4. Do NOT write any markdown table. The funding table is inserted automatically from verified data - writing your own means retyping figures from memory, and a previous draft that did so reported a round in the wrong currency.\n\n5. Do NOT write a Sources section. The appendix is generated from the verified claim list and appended automatically. Anything you write there would be a URL from memory.\n\n6. Every company name, dollar amount, date, and funding stage must match the analysis exactly. You are formatting evidence, not recalling it. Bold the specific numbers, dates, and company names, since partners scan for those first.\n\n7. Target 800-1100 words for the body. Cut rather than sprawl.\n\nReturn the title and the complete body markdown, Executive Summary through Investment Recommendation.",

      "expected_output": "A ReportDraft JSON object with exactly two keys: title (the investment question) and body_markdown (the full briefing, Executive Summary through Investment Recommendation, with no Sources section).",

      "output_pydantic": { "python": "report_refs.ReportDraft" },

      // Deterministic structure check: required sections present and ordered,
      // no hand-written Sources section, sane length, no promotional language.
      "guardrail": { "python": "report_refs.validate_report_draft" },
      "guardrail_max_retries": 2
    },

    {
      "name": "style_review_task",
      "agent": "style_reviewer",

      "context": ["format_report_task"],

      "description": "Review the draft briefing you have just been given against the house style guide, and return it corrected.\n\nHOUSE STYLE GUIDE:\n{style_guide}\n\nTHE ANALYSIS THE DRAFT WAS WRITTEN FROM - use it to check that nothing was invented, dropped, or altered:\n{analysis_block}\n\nWhat to check, in priority order:\n\n1. FACTUAL FIDELITY. Every company name, dollar figure, date, and funding stage in the draft must match the analysis. If the draft states something the analysis does not, remove it. This matters more than any style question - changing or inventing a number is the one unforgivable error here.\n\n2. Decisiveness. The guide is emphatic that a briefing which only describes the landscape has failed. Does it take a position? Does each recommendation state a concrete action and its main risk in the same breath?\n\n3. Specificity. Replace 'several startups' or 'a number of companies' with the named companies the analysis provides, or cut the sentence. Vague attribution is worse than a shorter report.\n\n4. Tone. No promotional language. No hedging every sentence about technical feasibility - the guide notes that reads as not having done the diligence rather than as appropriate caution.\n\n5. LENGTH. Count the words in the body. The target is 800-1100 and it is not advisory - a briefing that runs long stops being read, which defeats its purpose entirely. If the draft is over 1100 words, cutting it is a required edit, not an optional improvement. Tighten prose and drop the weakest supporting detail in each section. Never drop a required section, and never cut the risk statement from a recommendation. Only leave the body over 1100 words if the report explicitly says why it has to run long.\n\n6. Structure. Required sections, present and in the order the guide specifies.\n\nIf the draft genuinely meets the guide - including on length - return it unchanged with an empty revision_notes list. Do not invent changes to look diligent; unnecessary edits make documents worse. But do not mistake a well-written over-long draft for a compliant one.\n\nDo NOT add a Sources section or any markdown table; both are generated from verified data and inserted automatically.\n\nReturn the final title, the final body markdown, and a short list of what you changed and why.",

      "expected_output": "A ReviewedReport JSON object with exactly three keys: title, body_markdown (the final corrected briefing), and revision_notes (a list of strings describing what changed, which may be empty if nothing needed fixing).",

      "output_pydantic": { "python": "report_refs.ReviewedReport" },

      "guardrail": { "python": "report_refs.validate_reviewed_report" },
      "guardrail_max_retries": 2
    }
  ],

  "process": "sequential",
  "verbose": true,
  "memory": false,

  // Default kickoff inputs; all are overridden per run by ReportCrew.run().
  // Present so the config loads and can be inspected on its own.
  "inputs": {
    "topic": "small modular reactors",
    "style_guide": "(style guide loaded at runtime from knowledge/style_guide.md)",
    "sample_report": "(sample loaded at runtime from knowledge/prior_exec_report_sample.md)",
    "analysis_block": "(analysis rendered at runtime)",
    "funding_table": "(funding table rendered at runtime)"
  }
}
